00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:05.430 align:start position:0%
a strong but peaceful show of strength  by catholics in sri lanka  these people want justice for the 270  killed in six bomb attacks  on easter sunday in 2019.  seven of them were from teresa pierce's  family  her son her daughter and five  grandchildren  they went happily expecting to come back  but only their bodies returned  i'm asking that these issues are not  avoided by the authorities who are  enabling these tragedies to be repeated  eight suicide bombers detonated bombs in  churches and hotels  in a series of attacks they were blamed  on a local extremist group  affiliated to isil but many suspect  others were involved after two years of  waiting  the church is now campaigning for  answers  this is not a struggle between religions  race or politics  but it's a struggle of fear over unfair  light over darkness  justice over injustice the report of a  presidential inquiry into the bombings  was handed over in february  it says since 2015 the government did  not prioritize national security instead  prioritizing unity and reconciliation  witnesses said the government did not  take action against religious extremism  not only president sirisena and premier  riku masingay  but the entire government must take  responsibility we are more interested in  finding out who did it  who funded it and why did they do it the  church says black sunday  marks the first step of a journey to  secure justice  the bloodstains are long gone the qatar  peter church you see behind me  has been repainted but church leaders  say there will be no  closure until justice is served and the  identities of those behind  the easter bombings are revealed  mila fernandez al-jazeera katupitia
 

